<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-section-1-2">
      <title>Section 02 - Vectors in Component Form</title>
<introduction>
<objectives>
  <p><em>Learning Objectives</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Express a vector in component form.</li>
    <li>
      Find the magnitude of a vector and perform vector operations in component form.
    </li>
    <li>Find the component form of a vector using trigonometry.</li>
  </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
  Vectors can describe motion, force, and displacement, but working with them geometrically is not always the most efficient approach.
  By expressing vectors in component form, we translate geometric information into numerical form, which allows us to compute
  magnitudes, perform operations, and apply trigonometry in a clear and systematic way. This section introduces component form
  and shows how it connects points, directions, and vector operations.
</p>
</introduction>
      <subsection xml:id="sec-02-01-express-component">
        <title>Express a Vector in Component Form</title>
        <p>
  We begin by defining how a vector is written in component form.
</p>
        <definition>
  <title>Vectors in Component Form</title>
  <p>
    A vector <m>v = \langle x , y \rangle</m> with initial point
    <m>(x_i , y_i)</m> and terminal point <m>(x_t , y_t)</m>
    can be written in component form as
  </p>
  <p>
    <me>
      v = \langle x_t - x_i , y_t - y_i \rangle
    </me>
  </p>
  <p>
    The scalars <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are called the components of <m>v</m>.
    In particular, <m>x</m> is the first component and <m>y</m> is the second component.
  </p>
</definition>

<handout>
  <title>Concept Check</title>

  <checkpoint knowl="no">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Do not compute. Explain using words or a sketch. Suppose a vector starts at point
        <m>A</m> and ends at point <m>B</m>.
      </p>

      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            What does each component of the vector represent geometrically?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If two vectors have the same component form, what must be true about their
            magnitude and direction?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Can two different pairs of points produce the same component form?
            Explain.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </checkpoint>
</handout>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="sec-02-04-magnitude-ops">
        <title>Find the Magnitude of a Vector and Perform Vector Operations in Component Form</title>
       <p>
  Once vectors are written in component form, we can define their basic operations algebraically.
</p>
<definition>
  <title>Vector Operations</title>

  <p>
    Let <m>\mathbf{v} = \langle x_1 , y_1 \rangle</m> and
    <m>\mathbf{w} = \langle x_2 , y_2 \rangle</m> be vectors, and let
    <m>k</m> be a scalar.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        The magnitude of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
      </p>
      <me>
        \lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert = \sqrt{x_1^2 + y_1^2}
      </me>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        Scalar multiplication is defined by
      </p>
      <me>
        k \mathbf{v} = \langle k x_1 , k y_1 \rangle
      </me>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        Vector addition is defined by
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w} = \langle x_1 + x_2 , y_1 + y_2 \rangle
      </me>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        The magnitude of a scalar multiple satisfies
      </p>
      <me>
        \lVert k \mathbf{v} \rVert = |k| \lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert
      </me>
    </li>
  </ol>
</definition>
<worksheet xml:id="vector-magnitude-estimate">
  <title>In Class Example</title>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-magnitude-estimate-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Vector <m>\vec{v}</m> has initial point <m>(3,8)</m> and terminal point
        <m>(-4,-9)</m>. Find the magnitude of <m>\vec{v}</m>. Before computing,
        estimate whether the magnitude should be larger or smaller than <m>10</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
</subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-02-06-components-trig">
        <title>Find the Component of a Vector Using Trigonometry</title>
<definition>
  <title>Horizontal and Vertical Components of a Vector</title>

  <p>
    For a vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> with magnitude <m>\lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert</m> and
    direction angle <m>\theta</m>, the horizontal and vertical components of
    <m>\mathbf{v}</m> are given by
  </p>

  <me>
    \mathbf{v}
    =
    \langle
      \lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert \cos(\theta),
      \lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert \sin(\theta)
    \rangle
  </me>
</definition>
<worksheet xml:id="vector-components-trig-and-application">
  <title>In Class Example</title>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-components-trig-and-application-ex-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the vector <m>\vec{v}</m> with magnitude <m>6</m> that forms an angle of
        <m>150^\circ</m> with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Then find the component form
        of <m>\vec{v}</m>. Before finding components, predict whether the
        <m>x</m>-component of the vector should be positive, negative, or zero.
      </p>

        <image source="images/ch02/Example1.png">
          <description>
            A polar coordinate grid with degree markings shown alongside a Cartesian coordinate grid with horizontal and vertical axes.
          </description>
        </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-components-trig-and-application-ex-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A baseball player throws a ball at an initial speed of <m>105</m> mph at an angle
        of elevation of <m>35^\circ</m>. Determine the velocity vector of the baseball
        in component form. Round your answer to the nearest hundredth. Before computing,
        identify which component will be larger in magnitude and explain why.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
</subsection>
<subsection>
<title>Groupwork Activity</title>
<worksheet xml:id="vector-components-groupwork">
  <title>Exploring Vector Components</title>
<p>Work with a partner to answer the following questions:</p>
  <exercise xml:id="vector-components-groupwork-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Draw two different vectors that have the same magnitude but different component forms.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-components-groupwork-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Draw two vectors that have different magnitudes but share one component.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-components-groupwork-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A vector has component form <m>\langle 0 , y \rangle</m>.
        What can you say about its direction?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Justify each answer without performing calculations.
  </p>
</worksheet>
</subsection>
<subsection>
<title>Exercises on Vectors in Component Form</title>
       <worksheet xml:id="vector-component-practice">
  <title>Practice Problems</title>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-component-practice-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The initial and terminal points are given for the vector below.
        Express <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in component form.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Initial point <m>(1,2)</m>, terminal point <m>(3,4)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-component-practice-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle -6,12 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{w}=\langle -5,-8 \rangle</m>. Find the following.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>3\mathbf{v}+2\mathbf{w}</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\lVert -5\mathbf{w} \rVert</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-component-practice-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the points <m>P(-1,3)</m> and <m>Q(-1,8)</m>.
        Determine <m>\overrightarrow{QP}</m> in component form.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-component-practice-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The initial and terminal points are given for the vector below.
        Express <m>\mathbf{v}</m> in component form.
      </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Initial point <m>(-3,6)</m>, terminal point <m>(-8,-10)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-component-practice-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}=\langle -6,12 \rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{w}=\langle -5,-8 \rangle</m>.
        Find <m>-\tfrac12 \mathbf{w}-4\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="vector-component-practice-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the component form of a vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m> with magnitude <m>10</m>
        that forms an angle of <m>-41^\circ</m> with the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
        Round your answer to the nearest hundredth.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
</subsection>
    </section>
